1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,380
 Imagine your editing being so good that your viewers literally can't help but watch all the way to the end.

2
00:00:05,520 --> 00:00:06,820
 That's what I want for you, too.

3
00:00:06,980 --> 00:00:12,060
 So I took my years of experience in video editing and boiled it down into a formula with four pillars.

4
00:00:12,180 --> 00:00:18,500
 And you'll want to take notes today, because we're going way deeper than basics like cutting out the dead space.

5
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:20,680
 Editing is so much more than that.

6
00:00:21,560 --> 00:00:24,860
 And let's be honest, your video deserves so much more than that.

7
00:00:26,240 --> 00:00:31,100
 A great editing style can get you perfect watch time and millions of views,

8
00:00:31,380 --> 00:00:34,560
 but it can also get you terrible view counts and crushed dreams.

9
00:00:34,760 --> 00:00:37,680
 To figure out why that is, let's start with this video.

10
00:00:37,780 --> 00:00:38,020
 Period.

11
00:00:41,880 --> 00:00:46,480
 The video is literally just this guy Sam talking in his car and then working out.

12
00:00:47,060 --> 00:00:52,480
 It's 26 minutes long, but it only has 17 cuts and no other editing whatsoever.

13
00:00:52,480 --> 00:00:56,340
 How many views, how many subscribers do you think he has?

14
00:00:57,880 --> 00:01:01,080
 So does that mean editing is optional?

15
00:01:01,240 --> 00:01:01,560
 Right.

16
00:01:01,820 --> 00:01:02,160
 Hold on.

17
00:01:02,540 --> 00:01:03,000
 Slow down.

18
00:01:03,180 --> 00:01:04,120
 That's not what this is about.

19
00:01:04,360 --> 00:01:05,700
 Sam actually did edit.

20
00:01:05,900 --> 00:01:09,120
 He cut out all the boring parts his viewers didn't want to see.

21
00:01:09,300 --> 00:01:13,600
 And he left it at that, because his audience isn't looking for a super stimulating experience.

22
00:01:13,800 --> 00:01:16,640
 They just want to feel like they're hanging out with someone.

23
00:01:16,640 --> 00:01:22,660
 A bunch of fast cuts and flashy animations would kill the authentic experience the viewer came for.

24
00:01:22,840 --> 00:01:29,940
 And guess what, interrupting what the viewer actually came for is the fastest way to get them to click off.

25
00:01:30,160 --> 00:01:36,180
 While Sam averages 90 seconds between cuts, Mr. Beast does the exact opposite.

26
00:01:36,460 --> 00:01:39,580
 So what are you supposed to do when both editing styles work?

27
00:01:39,760 --> 00:01:43,260
 Start by thinking about the experience your viewers want.

28
00:01:43,260 --> 00:01:48,660
 Sam's edit shows us that millions of people come to YouTube to feel like they're hanging out with someone.

29
00:01:48,840 --> 00:01:53,240
 Mr. Beast's fast-paced style shows that a ton of people use YouTube purely for entertainment.

30
00:01:53,480 --> 00:01:56,460
 Every single one of your editing decisions feeds into that experience.

31
00:01:56,640 --> 00:02:03,200
 For example, if you cut out every pause so you're talking non-stop, that's by far the biggest thing you can do for entertainment.

32
00:02:03,500 --> 00:02:05,400
 But it comes at the cost of authenticity.

33
00:02:05,560 --> 00:02:08,580
 An effective editing style accounts for all of these decisions.

34
00:02:08,580 --> 00:02:17,860
 So while I walk you through the reasoning behind the edit on my first three uploads, take notes on what you should be adding to your own videos, based on what fits your audience best.

35
00:02:18,020 --> 00:02:20,520
 This video isn't a pile of tutorials.

36
00:02:20,620 --> 00:02:23,100
 It's a handbook for addictive editing.

37
00:02:23,320 --> 00:02:29,160
 Think of it as the foundation that tells you which tutorials are even worth watching.

38
00:02:30,440 --> 00:02:32,560
 I want you to take a look at this.

39
00:02:32,700 --> 00:02:33,740
 There's a problem here.

40
00:02:34,760 --> 00:02:38,280
 This is the timeline of a typically edited YouTube video.

41
00:02:38,440 --> 00:02:40,760
 A few cuts took out the unnecessary parts.

42
00:02:40,900 --> 00:02:44,920
 There was a tangent here that wasn't really relevant to the point of the video.

43
00:02:45,080 --> 00:02:47,760
 These cuts removed pauses and bad takes.

44
00:02:47,860 --> 00:02:52,180
 But as it stands, this video is doomed to never reach its potential.

45
00:02:52,320 --> 00:02:56,600
 And the reason is that it isn't using its visuals to maximize attention.

46
00:02:56,600 --> 00:02:57,720
 Think about it.

47
00:02:57,880 --> 00:03:02,060
 When you see a change in your field of view, like something moving suddenly, it grabs your attention.

48
00:03:02,200 --> 00:03:04,120
 The same thing happens when you're watching a video.

49
00:03:04,260 --> 00:03:07,900
 And here, there are only three different things to look at in an entire minute.

50
00:03:08,040 --> 00:03:09,020
 Kind of boring.

51
00:03:09,180 --> 00:03:15,900
 But if the video cut between A-roll, B-roll and other footage every few seconds, it would hold attention way better.

52
00:03:16,120 --> 00:03:19,560
 That said, there's also a downside to switching what's on screen too often.

53
00:03:19,560 --> 00:03:24,780
 Keep an eye out for that as we go through how the different types of footage should be used to maximize engagement.

54
00:03:24,920 --> 00:03:25,280
 A-roll.

55
00:03:25,280 --> 00:03:28,520
 This is footage where you can see and hear the subject at the same time.

56
00:03:28,760 --> 00:03:33,040
 It feels personal and holds attention really well when you're speaking confidently.

57
00:03:33,260 --> 00:03:36,040
 Use it in short bursts when you're saying something important.

58
00:03:36,220 --> 00:03:36,600
 B-roll.

59
00:03:36,600 --> 00:03:40,400
 This is extra footage that was shot separately from the audio.

60
00:03:40,560 --> 00:03:46,600
 It creates a visual representation of your words, so the viewer can actually see what you're talking about instead of just staring at your face.

61
00:03:46,600 --> 00:03:50,300
 It also lets you get things across much more clearly and much faster.

62
00:03:50,460 --> 00:03:53,720
 It's more interesting than A-roll, so use it as often as you possibly can.

63
00:03:53,960 --> 00:03:59,200
 A few variations of B-roll are stock footage, which is basically the easy version, and motion graphics.

64
00:03:59,200 --> 00:04:16,380
 Nope, I found a suspicious-looking math problem over by the pool table, and apparently I have to use the pool balls and the numbers on them to work out the solution.

65
00:04:16,440 --> 00:04:20,780
 Period. You really need to understand how crucial that editing decision was.

66
00:04:21,360 --> 00:04:25,080
 That was an important but boring moment in the story of the video.

67
00:04:25,380 --> 00:04:33,820
 Any attempt to use A-roll or regular B-roll in that moment would have been either too slow or horribly confusing, and either one would have cost viewers.

68
00:04:33,960 --> 00:04:36,840
 But an animation explained it crystal clear and fast.

69
00:04:37,220 --> 00:04:41,820
 That's the kind of editing decision you have to make to build a video people can't look away from.

70
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:47,260
 If you follow my channel, you've probably noticed that I've only uploaded three videos.

71
00:04:47,700 --> 00:04:52,460
 And with those I've gotten over 300,000 views and around 13,000 subscribers.

72
00:04:52,620 --> 00:04:56,820
 What makes it even crazier is that I didn't promote these videos anywhere.

73
00:04:57,000 --> 00:05:04,060
 I just uploaded them to YouTube and they took off completely on their own, thanks to the YouTube knowledge I've built up over the years.

74
00:05:04,580 --> 00:05:11,360
 Last year I started a channel that grew to 60,000 subscribers and over 24 million views.

75
00:05:12,360 --> 00:05:17,140
 Because of that, I've gotten a lot of comments and emails from people asking me for help.

76
00:05:17,760 --> 00:05:21,240
 So I wanted to let you know that I'm putting my calendar down below.

77
00:05:21,540 --> 00:05:23,280
 But it's only for serious YouTubers.

78
00:05:23,560 --> 00:05:29,400
 If that sounds interesting to you, you can book a call below to find out how to grow your YouTube channel.

79
00:05:29,600 --> 00:05:33,400
 By the way, spots are extremely limited right now, so don't miss out.

80
00:05:33,720 --> 00:05:38,900
 You probably remember from earlier that there's a downside to constantly cutting back and forth between different clips.

81
00:05:38,900 --> 00:05:43,060
 All that visual variety can turn into visual mush fast if you do it wrong.

82
00:05:43,240 --> 00:05:48,460
 Let's say you want to be engaging, so you try to cut between different clips as much as humanly possible.

83
00:05:48,620 --> 00:05:50,160
 You run the risk of making things confusing.

84
00:05:50,380 --> 00:05:52,060
 Which hurts engagement.

85
00:05:52,200 --> 00:05:56,140
 To avoid that, don't shorten your shots just for the sake of being short.

86
00:05:56,920 --> 00:05:57,260
 Period.

87
00:05:57,480 --> 00:06:03,540
 It's totally fine to leave the same clip on screen for 10 seconds if it's interesting for 10 seconds.

88
00:06:04,040 --> 00:06:09,020
 A lot of the time you don't even need to cut to a different clip to make your video more interesting.

89
00:06:09,120 --> 00:06:13,620
 Instead, use a few of these tricks to make the content way more immersive.

90
00:06:13,840 --> 00:06:16,880
 First, give boring still images some movement.

91
00:06:16,880 --> 00:06:19,760
 Either slowly change the scale or the position.

92
00:06:19,940 --> 00:06:22,880
 Or even better, make things more interesting with a change in perspective.

93
00:06:22,880 --> 00:06:27,700
 You can also make more abrupt scale changes to pull attention to specific things.

94
00:06:27,700 --> 00:06:35,380
 For example, when I'm saying something important in an A-roll segment, I zoom in close on my face to subtly tell the viewer to listen up.

95
00:06:35,540 --> 00:06:37,420
 You can also add shake keyframes.

96
00:06:37,600 --> 00:06:41,500
 Then you'll see images or text or maybe the whole video shaking.

97
00:06:41,920 --> 00:06:44,000
 You'll never catch me doing this, because I don't like it.

98
00:06:44,340 --> 00:06:46,540
 But if you want to do it, go for it.

99
00:06:48,360 --> 00:06:52,580
 Next, layer a bunch of visual elements over your footage.

100
00:06:54,280 --> 00:06:59,360
 A lot of people make the mistake of adding captions just to bump up the visual variety.

101
00:06:59,540 --> 00:07:02,200
 But don't forget the downside we talked about.

102
00:07:02,540 --> 00:07:09,260
 Using captions to fill more space can get ugly fast and waste a great opportunity to put something more engaging on screen.

103
00:07:09,420 --> 00:07:14,880
 So I only use captions when I want the viewer to catch specific words and pay extra attention to them.

104
00:07:15,040 --> 00:07:20,000
 But if there's genuinely nothing else you could put on screen, captions are often still better than nothing.

105
00:07:20,160 --> 00:07:24,020
 Also keep it to three words or fewer, since that makes them easier to read.

106
00:07:24,180 --> 00:07:26,600
 Let's say you're showing the viewer an image.

107
00:07:26,600 --> 00:07:31,840
 If you just throw the whole image up there, that's boring and, more importantly, confusing.

108
00:07:32,020 --> 00:07:38,780
 To make things crystal clear and add visual variety, direct the viewer's attention to the focal point of the image.

109
00:07:38,900 --> 00:07:43,360
 Here are six methods I use for that which go beyond just scaling and repositioning.

110
00:07:43,580 --> 00:07:47,880
 Take notes, because this is how you end up with professional-looking timelines like this one.

111
00:07:49,540 --> 00:07:54,440
 Animate in the most important line of text or the most important part of the image you're highlighting.

112
00:07:56,120 --> 00:08:00,720
 Darken or blur the area surrounding the focal point of the image.

113
00:08:02,360 --> 00:08:07,340
 Shift the image's color so it goes red, giving it a negative connotation.

114
00:08:07,500 --> 00:08:11,460
 You can also shift it to yellow or green to give it a positive spin.

115
00:08:11,580 --> 00:08:14,800
 Circles, arrows and underlines work too.

116
00:08:15,020 --> 00:08:17,820
 Oh, and one bonus: make the subject glow.

117
00:08:17,980 --> 00:08:21,500
 Another immersion hack is to simply make the video look good.

118
00:08:21,660 --> 00:08:26,020
 That can be color grading, adding a vignette, or some subtle particle animations.

119
00:08:26,140 --> 00:08:31,320
 And while we're on the subject of making things look good, let's focus on making them feel good.

120
00:08:31,320 --> 00:08:35,980
 This next pillar literally turns your video into a digital morphine drip.

121
00:08:37,080 --> 00:08:39,820
 So we've talked about visual variety.

122
00:08:40,200 --> 00:08:42,680
 But now let's talk about visual continuity.

123
00:08:42,840 --> 00:08:50,140
 If you can create a seamless flow of images, there are no rough edges, no spots where distractions can creep in.

124
00:08:50,320 --> 00:08:53,320
 There's no point where the viewer loses immersion.

125
00:08:53,320 --> 00:08:57,460
 The goal of this pillar is for every moment to flow seamlessly into the next.

126
00:08:58,380 --> 00:09:02,040
 The editing should be invisible, the experience captivating.

127
00:09:03,180 --> 00:09:07,260
 Any time a graphic shows up on screen, it can't just appear out of nowhere.

128
00:09:07,460 --> 00:09:10,240
 That doesn't make sense. It has to get into the frame somehow.

129
00:09:10,440 --> 00:09:13,200
 You'll find all of them in my free presets in the description.

130
00:09:13,380 --> 00:09:15,260
 You don't necessarily have to animate the elements.

131
00:09:15,440 --> 00:09:21,060
 You can also have something magically appear on screen, but then explain how it got there with a sound effect.

132
00:09:21,060 --> 00:09:25,880
 I like using this shutter sound effect, but a pop or something similar works too.

133
00:09:26,000 --> 00:09:27,460
 Now this part is extremely important.

134
00:09:28,380 --> 00:09:32,800
 I just made a cut, and in doing it I broke the most important rule of cutting.

135
00:09:33,640 --> 00:09:37,540
 In the last frame of that first clip, the viewer is looking me right in the eyes.

136
00:09:38,680 --> 00:09:44,440
 A tiny fraction of a second later, in the first frame of the second clip, the viewer is suddenly supposed to look over here.

137
00:09:44,640 --> 00:09:49,940
 That's a little jarring and it hurts the immersion your editing is supposed to be building.

138
00:09:49,940 --> 00:09:55,740
 Imagine the viewer having to bounce their eyes from one side of the screen to the other on every single cut.

139
00:09:55,860 --> 00:09:58,660
 That's a pretty unengaging viewing experience.

140
00:09:58,860 --> 00:10:03,020
 Another way to make transitions seamless is to use a full-screen transition.

141
00:10:03,160 --> 00:10:08,240
 There are tons of free packs out there, but if you like my subtle style, I've got something for you in the description.

142
00:10:08,360 --> 00:10:13,560
 And don't be afraid to break visual continuity once in a while, because remember, that jolts the viewer.

143
00:10:13,700 --> 00:10:15,360
 It'll grab their attention.

144
00:10:15,580 --> 00:10:16,920
 Take a look at this timeline.

145
00:10:17,040 --> 00:10:21,720
 We've talked about all these ways to make the video visually engaging.

146
00:10:21,980 --> 00:10:24,540
 But we've left out the other half of the video.

147
00:10:24,840 --> 00:10:28,600
 There's a completely different sense your viewer can experience the video with.

148
00:10:29,140 --> 00:10:38,960
 In pillar four, immersive audio and sound design makes the viewer experience every cut, every transition, every animation and every piece of footage with a second sense.

149
00:10:39,940 --> 00:10:46,060
 It's literally double the stimulation, and it can be used to steer the viewer's mood.

150
00:10:46,240 --> 00:10:51,280
 This is an engagement superpower that we'll get into in more detail when we get to your music choices.

151
00:10:51,280 --> 00:10:56,340
 But for now, the first step of sound design is building up a library of sounds to work with.

152
00:10:56,480 --> 00:10:57,660
 I've already done that for you.

153
00:10:57,800 --> 00:11:02,700
 Start with the free sound design pack of royalty-free sounds I put together for you in the description.

154
00:11:02,880 --> 00:11:06,620
 If you need more, check out freesound.org or grab Epidemic Sound.

155
00:11:06,800 --> 00:11:10,740
 Next, go through your video and just add sound effects everywhere it makes sense.

156
00:11:10,900 --> 00:11:13,360
 If something moves, you should add a whoosh.

157
00:11:13,500 --> 00:11:15,760
 And if something gets highlighted, a highlight sound.

158
00:11:15,960 --> 00:11:17,340
 Now here's the addictive part.

159
00:11:17,340 --> 00:11:23,420
 These next three types of sound effects literally hijack your viewers' emotions and pull them into the video.

160
00:11:23,540 --> 00:11:24,520
 First, risers.

161
00:11:24,760 --> 00:11:30,600
 They build tension and anticipation, because they tell the audience that something really important is about to happen.

162
00:11:30,800 --> 00:11:33,860
 But only use them when something important actually is about to happen.

163
00:11:34,200 --> 00:11:36,520
 Otherwise they lose their credibility and stop working.

164
00:11:36,660 --> 00:11:37,920
 Next up are hits.

165
00:11:38,100 --> 00:11:41,620
 These release tension and punctuate moments so they feel important.

166
00:11:41,780 --> 00:11:44,720
 You can use them on their own or put a riser in front of them.

167
00:11:44,900 --> 00:11:46,340
 It depends on the situation.

168
00:11:46,460 --> 00:11:48,260
 And again, don't overdo it.

169
00:11:48,440 --> 00:11:49,640
 Last but not least, tones.

170
00:11:49,840 --> 00:11:52,560
 These create a feeling of mystery and intrigue.

171
00:11:52,800 --> 00:11:56,760
 They're more for darker moods and they build a lot of underlying tension.

172
00:11:56,940 --> 00:12:01,060
 These sound effects work so well because they play on the viewer's emotions.

173
00:12:01,060 --> 00:12:06,680
 And that's powerful, because the more emotionally invested viewers are, the more engaged they are.

174
00:12:07,480 --> 00:12:10,340
 And these sound effects are just the tip of the iceberg.

175
00:12:11,080 --> 00:12:15,540
 Below the surface there's a huge amount of potential for emotional investment.

176
00:12:15,800 --> 00:12:16,760
 Your music choices.

177
00:12:16,960 --> 00:12:20,700
 For example, I like picking songs that create a sense of anticipation,

178
00:12:20,940 --> 00:12:24,880
 so viewers constantly feel like I'm about to say something really important.

179
00:12:24,880 --> 00:12:28,420
 Then, in a section where I'm giving game-changing advice,

180
00:12:28,620 --> 00:12:33,600
 I'll use a song with an innovative feel that builds a ton of excitement around what I'm saying.

181
00:12:33,760 --> 00:12:35,280
 It's really easy to pull off.

182
00:12:35,780 --> 00:12:38,340
 Split your video up by topic changes, then decide

183
00:12:38,600 --> 00:12:41,340
 what mood you want the viewer to be in for each topic.

184
00:12:41,520 --> 00:12:44,900
 By the way, you should put a lot of time into finding the right music,

185
00:12:45,160 --> 00:12:46,880
 because it really matters that it fits.

186
00:12:47,020 --> 00:12:50,640
 The YouTube Audio Library has a few good royalty-free songs,

187
00:12:50,640 --> 00:12:54,860
 and every now and then you'll find real gems from artists like Let Me Know who give their songs away for free.

188
00:12:54,860 --> 00:12:59,420
 But to be honest, subscribing to a licensed music platform is the best move

189
00:12:59,600 --> 00:13:01,060
 if you want to take YouTube seriously.

190
00:13:01,220 --> 00:13:05,180
 I'm not sponsored, but last time it saved me about four hours

191
00:13:05,180 --> 00:13:06,660
 and I found way better songs.

192
00:13:06,880 --> 00:13:09,220
 I've got an affiliate link for them in the description.

193
00:13:09,340 --> 00:13:13,140
 Okay, this is going to sound crazy, but there's even more you can do with your music choices

194
00:13:13,380 --> 00:13:15,200
 to pull the viewer into the video.

195
00:13:15,380 --> 00:13:18,360
 What do you think happens when you cut the music abruptly?

196
00:13:18,360 --> 00:13:22,060
 That sudden change jolts the viewer and grabs their attention.

197
00:13:22,220 --> 00:13:25,880
 On top of that, the absence of music pulls attention to other parts of the video.

198
00:13:26,100 --> 00:13:30,360
 So try pausing the music for special moments to make that moment stand out.

199
00:13:30,580 --> 00:13:33,560
 And what if you slowly faded the music out instead?

200
00:13:33,760 --> 00:13:37,420
 As soon as the viewer notices the music fading, they'll understand

201
00:13:37,800 --> 00:13:40,280
 that the section they're watching is coming to an end.

202
00:13:40,720 --> 00:13:43,500
 And that realization gives them anticipation for something new.

203
00:13:43,500 --> 00:13:46,180
 Okay, let's get a little more advanced.

204
00:13:46,380 --> 00:13:49,240
 Don't just match the mood of the song to the tone of the segment,

205
00:13:49,500 --> 00:13:54,840
 level up your music by syncing the highs and lows of the song to the video.

206
00:13:54,980 --> 00:13:58,200
 In this segment, for example, I introduced the problem right here

207
00:13:58,200 --> 00:14:00,260
 and started explaining the solution here.

208
00:14:00,640 --> 00:14:04,340
 I arrange things so the music drops in at exactly that moment.

209
00:14:05,500 --> 00:14:08,940
 That marks a clear topic change from problem to solution

210
00:14:08,940 --> 00:14:11,120
 and it also makes the solution segment more exciting.

211
00:14:11,180 --> 00:14:14,960
 And in case you're wondering why I'm playing four music tracks at once,

212
00:14:15,200 --> 00:14:18,720
 that's because I downloaded the individual stems of the song.

213
00:14:18,880 --> 00:14:22,280
 That's another Epidemic Sound feature that's really helpful.

214
00:14:22,400 --> 00:14:24,240
 Okay, I hope this helped.

215
00:14:24,420 --> 00:14:26,400
 There's a lot more that goes into keeping your audience hooked.

216
00:14:26,600 --> 00:14:28,500
 Click here to watch a video about it.

217
00:14:28,640 --> 00:14:31,840
 And if you're interested in that call, there's a link below.

